1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,120
I've been listening to a lot of videos about Grok Bot,

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:08,580
and the reason is because I'm a huge fan of Grok Bot and the support.

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,150
I'm a data engineer. I've worked with data bricks.

4
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:14,730
I've worked with other big data tools.

5
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:18,560
And on that journey, on that career path,

6
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:21,160
I've been encouraged to use AI at various points

7
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:24,340
and with various degrees of support.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:26,980
For example, first company I worked with,

9
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:29,400
I worked with AI with a, you know,

10
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:31,520
really, really encouraged everybody to use AI,

11
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:34,300
but then anytime you wanted to actually connect it to anything

12
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:39,640
to let it work autonomously or do anything without just being kind of an assistant.

13
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:46,160
You had to go through this AI use case review council

14
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:49,260
that was made up of all of these heads in the company.

15
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,760
It was like a six-month process,

16
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:55,400
and I was like, no, it's not worth my time to write up a brief

17
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:59,880
and go, like, make that pitch and do all that stuff just to get us connected.

18
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:03,970
You know, I didn't get to recognize a lot of the value that AI could bring.

19
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:08,220
And to be fair, at the time, it couldn't do everything that it could do now.

20
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:12,680
And right now, for me, Grok Bot is like,

21
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:15,420
Grok Bot feels like what I was promised with AI.

22
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:22,250
And so I just wanted to kind of talk through how I'm using Grok Bot

23
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:28,470
and then where I feel like I can continue to go with it.

24
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:34,360
And then, you know, maybe you get some tips here

25
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:38,770
or some things to help you perform in the data space

26
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:42,210
or to perform in any kind of engineering space.

27
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:47,980
So if you haven't heard of Grok Bot, I highly recommend looking it up.

28
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,040
Basically, the main thing for me is just that you don't have to manage the context windows

29
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:56,340
because you have these highly specialized bots that are all on the team together.

30
00:01:56,620 --> 00:02:00,600
They can all talk, they all share a VM, so they all technically have access

31
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,460
to the same information and processes and stuff like that.

32
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,040
But they'll respect each other's boundaries

33
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:08,240
even though they're operating on the same machine.

34
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:12,580
And then, usually, you'll just have one bot on top of all that

35
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:15,490
that can manage the rest of it for you

36
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:18,650
and can kind of hand off projects to individual bots

37
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:23,790
or, you know, just keep track of status across the sprawl

38
00:02:23,790 --> 00:02:27,290
because you'll see over time, like, yeah, you'll create a lot of bots.

39
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:29,550
They're really, really useful.

40
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:32,210
But the main thing that's been beneficial for me

41
00:02:32,210 --> 00:02:34,190
is just the ability to create that team.

42
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:37,720
So to give a little bit of background and context,

43
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:39,220
the role that I'm in right now,

